          "output": "**High-level summary:** \"Attention Is All You Need\" (Vaswani et al., Google Brain/Google Research/University of Toronto) introduces the **Transformer**, a sequence-transduction architecture built entirely on attention mechanisms—specifically self-attention—discarding the recurrence (RNNs/LSTMs) and convolutions that dominated prior models [Source: PDF, page 1; internal knowledge for the architecture claim].\n\n**What the supplied context supports:**\n- The paper's authorship and affiliations (title page) [Source: PDF, page 1].\n- Part of its motivation: a convolutional layer with kernel width k < n cannot connect all input/output position pairs, requiring stacks of O(n/k) (contiguous) or O(log_k(n)) (dilated) layers, which lengthens paths between positions—i.e., convolutions are costly for long-range dependencies [Source: PDF, page 7].\n\n**From internal knowledge (not verifiable in the supplied excerpts):** the paper argues recurrent models force sequential computation and struggle with long-range dependencies; proposes multi-head self-attention in an encoder–decoder stack; reports state-of-the-art BLEU on WMT 2014 English→German and English→French translation; and shows results on English constituency parsing.\n\n**Evidence gap:** The retrieved context contains only the title page and a fragment of the related-work/analysis section. The abstract, model description, and experimental results are not included, so specifics such as exact BLEU scores, layer counts, and the multi-head attention formulation cannot be confirmed from the supplied document. If you can provide those pages, I can give a fully sourced summary.",
